L = {}
L["ezra"] = """For about seventy years, the people of Judah had lived in exile in Babylon. Their city was in ruins and the temple was gone. Then the Persian empire took over, and King Cyrus let the Jews go home and rebuild. A first group returned and rebuilt the temple. Decades later, a priest and scribe named Ezra led a second group back to Jerusalem. The Bible says Ezra had set his heart to study the law of the Lord, to practice it, and to teach it to Israel.

The king of Persia gave Ezra letters, silver and gold, and the freedom to go. The trip was long and dangerous. Ezra was ashamed to ask the king for soldiers to protect them, because he had told the king that the hand of God was on everyone who looked to him. So instead they fasted and prayed for a safe journey, and God answered. They arrived in Jerusalem after about four months, with every piece of silver and gold accounted for.

A few days after he got there, Ezra's leaders came to him with bad news. Many of the people, including priests and Levites, had not kept themselves separate from the pagan peoples around them. They had married women from those nations and taken on their religious practices. The leaders themselves had been the worst in this. They were repeating the same mistakes that had sent their ancestors into exile in the first place.

When Ezra heard it, he tore his clothes and pulled hair from his head and beard. He sat down appalled. Everyone who trembled at the words of God gathered around him. He sat there until the evening sacrifice. Then, still in his torn clothes, he fell on his knees, spread out his hands to the Lord, and prayed.

My God, I am too ashamed and disgraced to lift up my face to you. Our sins are higher than our heads, and our guilt has reached to the heavens. Notice what he did. Ezra hadn't married a foreign woman. Yet he said, our sins, our guilt, we. He joined the people in confession instead of standing outside and pointing a finger.

He reminded God that he had been gracious. You have left us a remnant. You have given us a firm place in your holy place. Even in our slavery, you have not forsaken us. And then he said, Lord, God of Israel, you are righteous. We are left, a remnant, as in this day. Here we are before you in our guilt, though because of it, not one of us can stand in your presence.

While Ezra was praying and weeping and throwing himself down in front of the house of God, a very large crowd of men, women and children gathered around him. They wept bitterly too. One man, Shecaniah, spoke up. We have been unfaithful to our God, but in spite of this, there is still hope for Israel. Let us make a covenant before our God. Take courage and do it. Ezra got up and had the leaders swear to follow through. The people assembled in the rain, trembling over the matter, and they began to make things right.

Why does this matter? Ezra's prayer shows what real repentance looks like. It isn't a quick apology to get out of trouble. It is grief over sin, honesty about it, and trust in God's mercy. The people didn't wait for a program. One man's broken prayer started a turn around.

What happened after? Ezra later stood in front of the people at the Water Gate and read the law aloud from morning until noon. People who had been away for generations heard it, and they wept. Then the leaders told them it was a holy day, not a day for sadness. The joy of the Lord is your strength, they said. Ezra helped a whole nation remember who they were.

What does this mean for us today? When something is wrong in our family, church or community, we are tempted to blame others. Ezra shows another way. He took the problem to God first, and he spoke as someone who shared the guilt. It also shows that grief over sin is not the end. Ezra's prayer ends with honesty, but it began with God's mercy. And it shows that God's word has to be known to change people. Ezra didn't just pray. He studied, practiced and taught.

That is why this story is so important. It says that repentance is the way home. And it points to Jesus, the one who truly stands in for sinners before God, and who makes it possible for us to lift up our faces again."""

L["nehemiah"] = """Nehemiah lived in the Persian capital, far from Jerusalem. He had an important job. He was the king's cupbearer, the person who tasted the king's wine to make sure it wasn't poisoned. It meant he was close to the king and trusted by him. But his heart was far away in the city of his ancestors.

One day his brother Hanani arrived from Judah with a group of men. Nehemiah asked how things were in Jerusalem. They told him the people there were in great trouble and disgrace. The wall of Jerusalem was broken down, and its gates had been burned with fire. In those days, a city without walls was open to anyone. It was an easy target, and it was a shame to everyone who lived there.

When Nehemiah heard this, he sat down and wept. For days he mourned and fasted and prayed to God. His prayer was honest. He confessed his own sins and his family's sins. He reminded God of his promise to gather his scattered people back if they returned to him. Then he asked for one thing. Give your servant success today by granting him favor in the presence of this man. Nehemiah was talking about the king.

Four months later, Nehemiah was serving wine to King Artaxerxes when the king noticed his face. Why does your face look so sad when you are not ill? This can be nothing but sadness of heart. It was dangerous to look sad in front of the king, and Nehemiah was very afraid. But he answered carefully. May the king live forever. Why should my face not look sad when the city where my ancestors are buried lies in ruins?

The king said, what is it you want? Nehemiah prayed to the God of heaven. It was a quick prayer, in the moment, with the king waiting. Then he asked to be sent to Judah to rebuild the city. The king agreed. He even gave Nehemiah letters for safe passage and timber for the gates.

When Nehemiah arrived in Jerusalem, he didn't announce his plan. He waited three days. Then at night, he went out with a few men and rode around the city to inspect the damage. It was so bad that the animal he was riding couldn't get through in places. The next morning, he gathered the officials and said it plainly. You see the trouble we are in. Jerusalem lies in ruins, and its gates have been burned. Come, let us rebuild the wall of Jerusalem, and we will no longer be in disgrace. He told them how God had been gracious to him and how the king had helped. They answered, let us start rebuilding. So they began this good work.

Why does this matter? Nehemiah is a good example of how prayer and planning work together. He prayed for months, and he thought carefully about what he would need. When the moment came, he prayed again and acted. He didn't wait for someone else to fix the problem. He let it break his heart, and then he did something.

What happened after? The work had enemies. Men named Sanballat and Tobiah laughed at the Jews and then threatened them. Nehemiah put half of his workers on guard with spears while the other half built, and the builders carried tools in one hand and a weapon in the other. In spite of all the opposition, the wall was finished in just fifty-two days. Even their enemies had to admit that the work was done with the help of God.

What does this mean for us today? Is there something broken that you can't stop thinking about? A relationship, a neighborhood, a church, a part of your own life. Nehemiah suggests that this kind of burden may be where God is calling us to act. Start with prayer, be honest about the problem, and ask for what you need. Then take the first step. And expect opposition. Good work often draws critics. Keep going anyway.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God uses ordinary people in ordinary jobs to rebuild what is broken. And it points to Jesus, who left a place of comfort and came to a broken world to rebuild it from the ground up."""

L["esther"] = """Esther was a young Jewish woman living in the Persian empire. Her parents had died, and her cousin Mordecai raised her as his own daughter. When King Xerxes got rid of his queen and held a search for a new one, Esther was taken to the palace. She won the king's favor, and he made her queen. Mordecai had told her not to tell anyone that she was Jewish, and she kept it secret.

The king's highest official was a man named Haman. He was proud and demanded that everyone bow to him. Mordecai refused. Haman was furious, and when he found out Mordecai was a Jew, he wasn't satisfied with punishing one man. He wanted to destroy all the Jews in the empire. He went to the king and told him that there was a certain people scattered through his kingdom whose customs were different and who didn't obey the king's laws. He offered a huge sum of money for the job. The king gave him his signet ring, and a decree went out to every province. On a single day, all the Jews, young and old, women and children, were to be destroyed.

Mordecai put on rough cloth and ashes and wept in the street. When Esther heard about it, she sent clothes to him, but he sent back a message. Tell her to go to the king and beg for mercy for her people. Esther answered that anyone who approached the king without being called could be put to death, unless the king held out his golden scepter. She hadn't been called in thirty days.

Mordecai's reply is among the most famous lines in the Bible. Do not think that because you are in the king's house you alone of all the Jews will escape. If you remain silent at this time, relief and deliverance for the Jews will arise from another place, but you and your father's family will perish. And who knows but that you have come to your royal position for such a time as this?

Esther made her decision. Go, gather all the Jews in Susa, and fast for me. Do not eat or drink for three days. My attendants and I will fast as you do. When this is done, I will go to the king, even though it is against the law. And if I perish, I perish.

On the third day, Esther put on her royal robes and stood in the inner court of the palace, in view of the king. When the king saw Queen Esther standing there, he was pleased with her and held out the gold scepter. She approached and touched the tip of it. The king asked, what is it, Queen Esther? What is your request? Even up to half the kingdom, it will be given to you.

Esther didn't tell him everything right then. She invited the king and Haman to a banquet. At the second banquet, she told the king. If I have found favor with you, my king, grant me my life, and spare my people. For I and my people have been sold to be destroyed. The king asked, who is he? Where is the man who has dared to do such a thing? Esther said, the adversary and enemy is this vile Haman.

Why does this matter? The book of Esther never mentions God by name, yet his hand is all over it. A young orphan becomes queen, a sleepless night leads the king to remember what Mordecai had done for him, and a plot turns against its author. God works through timing and through people who choose to be brave.

What happened after? Haman was removed, and Mordecai was given his position. The king couldn't cancel his own decree, but he allowed the Jews to defend themselves. The people were rescued. To this day, the Jewish festival of Purim celebrates this deliverance.

What does this mean for us today? We may not face a king's palace, but we all have moments when we could speak up and don't. Esther's story asks whether we are where we are for such a time as this. It also shows that courage doesn't mean not being afraid. Esther was terrified. She prayed, fasted, got help from her community, and then went in anyway.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God protects his people, even when he seems hidden. And it points to Jesus, who laid down his life to save his people, and who also said what Esther said, and meant it more than anyone."""

L["job"] = """There was a man named Job who lived in the land of Uz. The Bible says he was blameless and upright, and that he feared God and turned away from evil. He had seven sons and three daughters, thousands of animals, and many servants. He was the greatest man among all the people of the East. Job also took care of his family spiritually. He would offer sacrifices for his children after their feasts, just in case they had sinned in their hearts.

The book opens with a scene in heaven. The angels came to present themselves before the Lord, and Satan, the accuser, came with them. God pointed to Job as an example. Have you considered my servant Job? There is no one like him. Satan answered that Job only served God because God had blessed him. Take away what he has, and he will curse you to your face. God allowed Satan to test Job, but only up to a point.

Then one day, the worst news arrived in waves. A messenger came running to Job and said that raiders had stolen his oxen and donkeys and killed his servants. While he was still speaking, another messenger came. Fire had fallen from the sky and burned up the sheep and shepherds. While he was still speaking, a third came. Raiders had taken the camels. And while he was still speaking, a fourth messenger arrived with the worst news of all. Your sons and daughters were having a feast at their oldest brother's house, and a mighty wind swept in from the desert and struck the house. It collapsed on them, and they are dead. I am the only one who escaped to tell you.

In one day, Job lost his wealth and all ten of his children. He stood up and tore his robe. He shaved his head. Then he fell to the ground in worship. Naked I came from my mother's womb, and naked I will depart. The Lord gave and the Lord has taken away. May the name of the Lord be praised. In all this, Job did not sin by charging God with wrongdoing.

Then Satan struck Job's health. Painful sores covered his body from head to foot, and Job sat among the ashes and scraped his skin with a piece of broken pottery. His wife said, are you still maintaining your integrity? Curse God and die. Job answered, you are talking like a foolish woman. Shall we accept good from God and not trouble?

Three of Job's friends came to comfort him. At first they did it right. They sat on the ground with him for seven days and seven nights without saying a word. But then they started to talk. They insisted that Job must have done something terrible to deserve this. Job knew he hadn't. He argued, he cried out, and he demanded that God answer him.

Why does this matter? The book of Job faces a hard question honestly. Why do good people suffer? It doesn't give a neat answer. When God finally speaks, he doesn't explain the reason. He shows Job the vastness of creation and asks, where were you when I laid the earth's foundation? Job is humbled. He says, my ears had heard of you, but now my eyes have seen you.

What happened after? God told the friends that they had not spoken what was right about him, and Job prayed for them. God gave Job twice as much as he had before. He had seven more sons and three more daughters. He lived another 140 years. But the point of the book isn't the reward at the end. It's that Job held on to God in the dark.

What does this mean for us today? Suffering isn't always a punishment, and a friend's best comfort is often just showing up and sitting quietly. Job also shows that honest complaint is allowed. He didn't hide his pain, and God didn't reject him for it. And Job said something in the middle of his misery that has held up many people since. I know that my Redeemer lives.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God is trustworthy even when we can't understand what's going on. And it points to Jesus, the one innocent person who suffered the most, and who understands our pain from the inside."""

L["isaiah"] = """The year King Uzziah died was a time of fear in Judah. Uzziah had been a strong king for fifty-two years, and now he was gone. The nation had no certainty about what came next. In that year, a young man named Isaiah went to the temple, and what he saw there changed his life.

Isaiah saw the Lord, high and exalted, seated on a throne. The train of his robe filled the temple. Above him were seraphim, angelic beings, each with six wings. With two wings they covered their faces, with two they covered their feet, and with two they were flying. And they were calling to one another, holy, holy, holy is the Lord Almighty. The whole earth is full of his glory. The doorposts and thresholds shook at the sound of their voices, and the temple was filled with smoke.

Isaiah's reaction wasn't excitement. It was dread. Woe to me, he cried. I am ruined. For I am a man of unclean lips, and I live among a people of unclean lips, and my eyes have seen the King, the Lord Almighty. When he stood in front of a perfectly holy God, he saw his own sin clearly. He was in the presence of the one he had spoken about for years, and he knew he didn't measure up.

Then one of the seraphim flew to him with a burning coal in his hand. He had taken it with tongs from the altar. He touched Isaiah's mouth with it and said, see, this has touched your lips. Your guilt is taken away and your sin atoned for. Isaiah hadn't earned it. He hadn't even asked for it. God dealt with his sin himself.

Then Isaiah heard the voice of the Lord. Whom shall I send? And who will go for us? Isaiah didn't hesitate. Here am I. Send me. It's the response of a man who has been forgiven and can't help but say yes.

God's assignment was a hard one. Go and tell this people, be ever hearing but never understanding. Be ever seeing but never perceiving. Many of the people wouldn't listen. Isaiah asked, for how long, Lord? God said, until the cities lie ruined and without inhabitant. But God also gave him a promise. As the terebinth and oak leave stumps when they are cut down, so the holy seed will be the stump in the land. There would always be a remnant, a seed from which God would start over.

Why does this matter? The vision shows what God is like. He is holy, which means completely set apart, completely good, completely other. Isaiah's story shows that meeting God makes us aware of our sin, but God doesn't leave us there. He provides the cleansing himself. And only then does he send.

What happened after? Isaiah served as a prophet for about sixty years, under four kings. He warned Judah and comforted them. He wrote some of the most beautiful and hopeful words in the Bible, including promises about a child to be born. For to us a child is born, to us a son is given, and the government will be on his shoulders. And he will be called Wonderful Counselor, Mighty God, Everlasting Father, Prince of Peace. He also wrote about a servant who would be pierced for our transgressions and by whose wounds we are healed.

What does this mean for us today? Many of us want to be used by God but feel too flawed. Isaiah felt that too, and God's answer was to forgive him first. We don't have to be perfect to be sent. We have to be cleansed and willing. It also shows that saying yes to God may not mean quick success. Isaiah was told that many would not listen. He went anyway.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God is holy, and that he is also the one who cleanses us and calls us. And it points to Jesus. The apostle John said that Isaiah saw Jesus' glory and spoke about him, and it's Jesus who takes away our guilt for good."""
